  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Uganda Chronic Crisis III</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Arua and Madi Okollo districts, North Western Uganda</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">As part of the previous Chronic Crisis II project, an enhanced Vulnerability and Capacity Assessment (eVCA) was conducted in 2023 in which the targeted communities prioritized floods, droughts, environmental degradation, food insecurity, and epidemics caused by poor sanitation and hygiene as priority risks. Given that further decrease in international funding to support communities at risk of these crises is expected, in this third and final phase of Chronic Crisis investment in Arua and Madi Okollo Districts will focus on strengthening community resilience to these priority risks to prepare for and manage future hazards.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Chronic Crisis III project, a collaborative effort between Uganda Red Cross Society (URCS) and Netherlands Red Cross (NLRC), aims to strengthen the resilience of 70.348 refugees and host community members in Arua and Madi Okollo districts of Uganda to water-related risks, including flood and drought. The project builds upon previous interventions in the region and addresses the ongoing challenges faced by communities in a protracted refugee crisis, exacerbated by climate change impacts. The project focuses on two key outcomes: improving community capacity for early warning and preparedness to disasters, and enhancing access to and use of water, sanitation, and hygiene services. These objectives will be achieved through a range of activities, including strengthening Community-Based Disaster Risk Reduction (CBDRR) groups, implementing community action plans, rehabilitating water sources, improving sanitation facilities, and promoting environmental conservation.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The project emphasizes a participatory approach, building on existing community structures and capacities, and integrating institutional and community-level disaster preparedness. Cross-cutting themes such as Protection, Gender and Inclusion (PGI), Community Engagement and Accountability (CEA), Green Response, and National Society Development are integrated throughout the project.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The project intends to continue the provision of life-saving services to a total of 70.348 people, of which 26.568 refugees in Zone 4 of Rhino settlement, and 70.348 in host community members in KamKam villages, Ajia and Arivu in Madi Okollo and Arua District, including children, adults and the elderly.</narrative>
  </description>
